id: k8s.reconciliation
kind: kubernetes
title: Reconciliation loop
aliases: reconcile, control loop, convergence
tags: architecture, controllers
questions:
  - how does a Kubernetes controller work?
  - how would you write a controller?
invariant: A loop that reads desired state, reads observed state, computes a diff and acts, then repeats -- never a one-shot command sequence.
answer_30s: Reconcile reads desired and observed state, computes the diff and performs the minimum action to close it, then gets called again on the next event.
answer_2m: |
  Write it as: observe current state; compare with what the spec declares; act
  on the difference; emit the new status; return. Two properties make it safe:
  idempotence (running it twice with no external change does nothing) and
  level-triggered thinking (react to the state you observe, not to the event
  that woke you, so a missed event is not a lost update). Failures are recorded
  in status and retried with exponential backoff rather than blocking the loop.
  This same pattern is why the primitive graph is written as `watch -> diff ->
  act -> report` in the recipes: it is the general shape of every system that
  keeps two descriptions of the world in sync.
when_to_use:
  - any controller, operator, or system that must keep an external resource in sync
commands:
  - kubectl get <kind> -o yaml => spec is desired, status is observed; the gap is the work
failure_modes:
  - edge-triggered logic that assumes no event was ever missed
  - non-idempotent action, so retries duplicate work
follow_ups:
  - how do you test a reconcile loop?
related:
  - k8s.desired-state
  - k8s.controller
  - design.idempotency
  - design.retry-backoff
  - recipe.controller
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/controller/
  - https://book.kubebuilder.io/architecture.html
